d{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation \n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7.5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=7.5+3x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186271958 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271958 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139646" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271958 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271958 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $5$ | $7$ | $12$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{56}\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}25 \\\\ 28\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186271958 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "64195b27-b671-5dc0-b27c-842f1fe0fae2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271958 #edb/ref 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 