X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186271966 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a70d052-0349-524f-90c0-17395372863f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271966 #edb/ref 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 8.5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6.5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}38 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}44 \\\\ 250\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}17.2 \\\\ -1.4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=17.2-1.4x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the selling price of a car of the same model that is $10$ years old, we substitute $x=10$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =17.2-1.4(10) \\\\  & =3.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the sale price will be $$3\\,200.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=10$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186324462 #edb/ref 1080 "$$29.8$ million" #edb/re