[17592186326903 #edb/ref 1069 "q-41467" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326903 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326903 #edb/ref 1071 "The relationship between the advertising budget and sales (both in millions of dollars) of a company per year over several years is shown in the following table. \n\n| Advertising $(x)$ | **$2$** | **$2.5$** | **$3$** | **$3.5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Sales $(y)$ | $10$ | $12$ | $16$ | $20$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the total sales when the annual advertising budget is equal to $$5$ million, assuming the trend continues? \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}31.5 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}58 \\\\ 168\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186326903 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3832f676-5ec9-5b13-afe4-f7324769e216" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326903 #edb/ref 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3.5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}31.5 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}58 \\\\ 168\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4.2 \\\\ 6.8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-4.2+6.8x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the value of the sales when the advertising budget is equal to $$5$ million, we substitute $x=5$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4.2+6.8(5) \\\\  & =29.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the total sales when the advertising budget equals $$5$ million will be $$29.8$ million. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=5$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186369755 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186369756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369755 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139626" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369755 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369755 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 32\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186369755 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "59dee5e8-59ff-54dc-b1c9-fe33f65f9a33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369755 #edb/ref 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 32\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2.8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-2.8+2x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186369756 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369756 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369756 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369756 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369756 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369756 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369756 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369756 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186369761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369756 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b9aa9c57-0f30-5df5-9270-ceb54bd9fc0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369757 #edb/ref 1080 "y=-2.8-2x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369757 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369757 #edb/ref 1083 